e long-term environmental consequences of human dependence on marine ecosystems.","confidence":0.98},"academicOrientation":{"score":65,"confidence":0.86},"profileConfidence":0.93,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"234cdcb1899f2b6711f25c74c6b7c35dc65bb950fdc79b20aa69b57208b371e9","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["central_places_hit_schema_cap"]},"primarySubject":"Nature & Environment","secondarySubjects":["American History","History"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily examines the environmental history of the North Atlantic and the impact of fishing practices over centuries, making it best categorized under Nature & Environment, with relevant ties to American History due to its focus on American waters.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"a8e92b47c84ca7c92395dcb04d2c8e174762d9b69948a640539f199c6167bc76","summary":"Winner of the Bancroft Prize A sweeping environmental history of the North Atlantic, The Mortal Sea reveals how centuries of fishing turned a rich ocean into an “exhausted sea” and offers a powerful cautionary tale about our precarious relationship with the natural world. Since the Viking ascendancy in the Middle Ages, the Atlantic has shaped the lives of people who depend upon it for survival. And just as surely, people have shaped the Atlantic. In his innovative account of this interdependency, W. Jeffrey Bolster, a historian and professional seafarer, takes us through a millennium-long environmental history of our impact on one of the largest ecosystems in the world. While overfishing is often thought of as a contemporary problem, Bolster reveals that humans were transforming the sea long before factory trawlers turned fishing from a handliner’s art into an industrial enterprise. The western Atlantic’s legendary fishing banks, stretching from Cape Cod to Newfoundland, have attracted fishermen for more than five hundred years. Bolster follows the effects of this s